1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,780
You're building a rag application, but the embedding model is eating half your latency and GPU budget,

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,960
and you're starting to wonder, what would you actually lose by going smaller?

3
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:12,720
Well, that's the question behind Jinaiai's new V5 text embedding models.

4
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:18,820
The two models, the V5 small and the V5 nano, compete head-to-head with models many times their size,

5
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:21,360
while being state-at-the-art in their own size class.

6
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:26,480
The Jinaii team also released the paper with a ton of detail about their two-stage training approach.

7
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:32,600
They combined knowledge distillation with task-specific laura adapters and they're refreshingly open

8
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,120
about what worked and what didn't.

9
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:36,990
First, the key facts.

10
00:00:37,550 --> 00:00:45,750
The GINA V5Tex small is a 677 million parameter model with a 32K token context length and 1,024-dimension embeddings.

11
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:53,950
The V5Tex Nano model has 239 million parameters with an 8K context length and 768-dimensional embeddings.

12
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:56,890
Both include four tasks-specific.

13
00:00:56,930 --> 00:01:01,530
law adapters that let you pick a specialized version of each model at inference time,

14
00:01:01,790 --> 00:01:04,630
asymmetric retrieval, text-matching, clustering or classification.

15
00:01:05,730 --> 00:01:09,970
Both were trained with Matrioshka representation learning and optimized for binary quantization.

16
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:16,470
The small model is based on Quent3, 0.6 billion base, the nano is based on Eurobird 210 million.

17
00:01:16,930 --> 00:01:21,970
Both base models are multilingual, and the GNA models were further trained on a corpus spanning 30 languages.

18
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:24,030
So how do they actually perform?

19
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:26,980
Both models lead or neal lead their size.

20
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,380
class on the MTAB. The V5 small scores 71.

21
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:37,420
7, while the nano scores 71. Now to put that in perspective, the nano at 239 million

22
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:43,020
parameters is outperforming the quen 3.6b, which is more than twice its size. And both are

23
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:47,620
within a couple of points of the 4 billion parameter quen 3. The multilingual mtab tells

24
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:54,980
the same story. The V5 small at 67.0 or the V5 nano at 65.5 are top of the class and not

25
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:56,140
that far off the much larger models.

26
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,720
Gina's V5 models are trained for asymmetric retrieval, meaning that they're designed to fetch

27
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:05,760
the right documents based on much shorter queries. Beyond the MTAB, the authors tested against

28
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:11,100
three additional retrieval benchmarks, RTAB, BEO, and Long Embed, each probing different aspects

29
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:15,520
of these models. But the picture is consistent. There are a few cases where specific competitors

30
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:20,020
edge ahead, like the Quame 3.6 billion on English MTAB and Long Embed, and the Voyage 4

31
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:26,180
Nano on a few multilingual benchmarks. But the Voyage model outputs quite large embeddings at 2048 dimensions,

32
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:28,220
so it's not quite an apples-to-apples comparison.

33
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:34,140
The key takeaway is neither V5 model has a weak spot with abnormally low scores on any benchmarks,

34
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:36,840
which makes them a very safe, versatile choice.

35
00:02:37,540 --> 00:02:41,740
Both models are trained with Matriushka representation learning to allow output embedding truncation

36
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:43,260
down to 32 dimensions.

37
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:45,840
And the performance loss really isn't bad.

38
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:46,820
Take a look at this.

39
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:51,280
Until you get to below 256 dimensions, the loss is relatively minimal.

40
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:56,160
So in many cases, you can significantly reduce your vector index footprint just by short

41
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:59,120
your embedding length without giving up much retrieval quality.

42
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:05,040
Now, what about quantization? Well, the authors tested binary quantization, which reduces the precision

43
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:10,340
of each embedding dimension from a floating point number to a single bit. Even with that massive

44
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:15,220
reduction in size, the V5 models only lost a couple of points on their benchmark scores, which is

45
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:19,100
really impressive. So if you're running on constrained infrastructure, like a 4 gigabyte

46
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:25,960
VPS, you could go nano, truncate to 256 dimensions, and apply binary quantization, and still have a very

47
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:31,060
capable retrieval system at a tiny footprint. We've seen the aggregate multilingual numbers,

48
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:36,000
but the more useful view, in my opinion, is the per-language breakdown. Each square here in

49
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,880
this grid shows how well the V5 small performs compared to other similar models. The green

50
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:45,920
the better, the red are the worse. This is the kind of chart worth bookmarking. Say you're building

51
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:49,960
a multilingual right pipeline and your knowledge base has documents in English, German, and

52
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:55,260
Japanese. You look at this grid and you'll see green across the board for these languages. You'd be well-served.

53
00:03:55,260 --> 00:04:00,400
But say your corpus is heavily Romanian. You see some red here, and it'll be worth evaluating

54
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:05,940
alternatives for that specific use case. That honesty in the result is actually useful. It helps

55
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:10,800
you make a real decision rather than just trusting a single aggregate number. The architecture

56
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:15,720
is a standard transform-based embedding model. You feed in text, it passes through the transformer

57
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:21,320
layers, and the embedding of the end-of-sequence token becomes the output using last token pooling.

58
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:25,400
Notice those law adapters that we mentioned earlier. At inference time,

59
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:29,600
you pick the adapter that matches your task. You can see the retrieval adapter highlighted here,

60
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:33,220
and the model loads these lightweight adapter weights on top of the base model.

61
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:38,280
For local deployment, Gina provides standalone weight files with the Laura weights pre-merged,

62
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:41,880
so you don't have to deal with conditional adapter loading yourself. They've also released

63
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:47,140
14 GGUF quantization variants for each model so you can pick the right-size performance tradeoff

64
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:52,380
for your infrastructure. And the models work with popular inference tools like VLM and Lama

65
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:55,520
CPP for production deployments. These models,

66
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:59,840
models are also already available in GNIR's inference API, as well as the Elastic Inference

67
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:02,500
Service, so we can try them out with minimal setup overhead.

68
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:05,980
The V5GNA models were trained with the two-stage process.

69
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:11,900
The first step transfers knowledge from the larger QEN3 embedding 4 billion teacher model to the student.

70
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:17,420
It works like this. Both models process the same text pairs to produce embeddings, things like

71
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:22,460
the title abstract pairs or question answer pairs. Then the student is continually updated

72
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:25,820
to minimize the distance between its embeddings and the teachers.

73
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:27,920
There's a practical problem though.

74
00:05:28,580 --> 00:05:32,440
The Quinn3 teacher model outputs 2,560-dimensional vectors,

75
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:35,960
while the V5 small model outputs 1,024-dimensional vectors.

76
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:38,240
So you need a projection layer to bridge the gap.

77
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:42,780
A projection layer is like a translator that allows two embedding spaces to be compared.

78
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:46,660
Here the authors had to decide whether the layer would project an embedding from a student,

79
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:48,580
opposite to teacher, or vice versa,

80
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:51,700
and whether to freeze that projection layer during training.

81
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:58,820
They tested all four combinations and found that projecting the teachers' embeddings down with an unfrozen projection layer works very poorly.

82
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:05,120
The projection layer absorbs all the learning and becomes a shortcut, while the student model itself barely improves.

83
00:06:05,530 --> 00:06:10,730
The best approach is in fact the opposite, project the student model up to the teacher's space,

84
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:14,230
and let both the projection layer and the student model update together.

85
00:06:14,930 --> 00:06:15,710
And one more detail.

86
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:19,650
The initial distillation didn't work well but retrieving long documents.

87
00:06:20,090 --> 00:06:21,750
The authors fixed this with additional training.

88
00:06:21,770 --> 00:06:26,570
on a curated data set. So synthetic documents with key information hidden in noisy text,

89
00:06:27,090 --> 00:06:31,690
plus long book chapters and articles paired with short queries. They highlight that this

90
00:06:31,690 --> 00:06:37,210
extra training phase was key to the model's long document retrieval performance. The paper goes deeper

91
00:06:37,210 --> 00:06:42,910
into specifics if you're interested. After distillation, they froze the base model weights and trained

92
00:06:42,910 --> 00:06:47,970
the four laura adapters, each with its own loss function and training data. The good thing about these

93
00:06:47,970 --> 00:06:51,750
laura adapters is that each of them is only on 3 to 4% of the base model.

94
00:06:51,770 --> 00:06:56,800
size, so this is much faster. They trained the retrieval adapter by retaining the same

95
00:06:56,800 --> 00:07:02,520
distillation loss from the first stage and adding to other loss functions. First, a contrastive loss

96
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:06,640
with hard negatives. This is what teaches the model to distinguish between results that are

97
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:13,380
semantically close, but not actually relevant. Second, a global orthogonal regularizer or GOR loss.

98
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:19,220
This is why the model retains so much quality on the binary quantization. The author's going to further

99
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:21,940
detail on the text matching, clustering and classification adaption.

100
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:25,780
and if you're interested in training methods for these tasks, it's well worth the read.

101
00:07:26,670 --> 00:07:31,530
So, to wrap up, what you get from this two-stage approach is genuinely impressive for its size.

102
00:07:31,670 --> 00:07:37,470
The V5 small matches the 3.8 billion parameter GNAV4 model on retrieval at one-sixth

103
00:07:37,470 --> 00:07:41,750
size, and the V5 nano model outperforms models twice as large.

104
00:07:42,450 --> 00:07:47,670
Add in long context support, strong multilingual coverage, matrioshka truncation and quantization

105
00:07:47,670 --> 00:07:51,970
robustness, and these are models you can confidently deploy across wide range of use cases.

106
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:57,130
They're available on Hugging Face, the Gina AI API, and the Elastic Inference Service.

107
00:07:57,710 --> 00:08:00,230
Just note the license details if you're considering commercial use.

108
00:08:00,690 --> 00:08:04,470
What's also interesting is where this leaves the efficiency curve. If models this small

109
00:08:04,470 --> 00:08:08,710
can perform at this level today, the next generation of distillation techniques should be really

110
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:13,330
something. Okay then, thanks for watching. Links to further resources are in the description,

111
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:18,250
and don't forget to hit like and subscribe to help others find us more easily. See you then.
